# Normalise line endings in the repository. Without this, Git on Windows converts LF to
# CRLF in the WORKING TREE (the warnings it prints on the first `add`), and a file's checked-
# out bytes then depend on who cloned it. These plugins are loaded straight from disk by the
# app and diffed by tests, so a platform-dependent working tree is a real source of noise.
#
# `text=auto` stores LF in the repository and checks out the platform default. The explicit
# rules below cover files whose bytes matter:

* text=auto

# PowerShell: Windows PowerShell 5.1 decodes .ps1 using the ANSI code page, so these are kept
# as CRLF to match how they were authored and tested. `dsh-restart.ps1` is documented as
# pure ASCII for the same reason.
*.ps1 text eol=crlf
*.cmd text eol=crlf

# Shell-level scripts and source: LF.
*.js  text eol=lf
*.mjs text eol=lf
*.json text eol=lf
*.yml text eol=lf
*.md  text eol=lf

# Binary or generated: never touch.
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.zstd binary
